#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130340 :tutorial/content "To raise the product of two numbers to a power, we can raise each number to that power, and then multiply. This is the **power of product rule for exponents**.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression $(5 \\times 3)^4$ by applying the power of product rule:\n\n$$\n(5\\times 3)^4=5^4\\times 3^4\n$$\n\n**Note:** The power of product rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(5 \\cdot 3)^{4} &=  \\\\\n(5 \\cdot 3) \\cdot (5 \\cdot 3) \\cdot (5 \\cdot 3) \\cdot (5 \\cdot 3)_(⏟)_(4copies) &=  \\\\\n(5 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 5)_(⏟)_(4copies) \\cdot (3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3)_(⏟)_(4copies) &=  \\\\\n&= 5^{4} \\cdot 3^{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "79b0e407-5948-563e-a8af-5dbcd3ab59f3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3272 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
